12489: 
12490: (5) Operand TLB Multiple-Hit Exception
12491: 
12492: � Source: Multiple UTLB address matches
12493: � Transition address: H'A000 0000
12494: � Transition operations:
12495: 
41415:                                                                               Section 20 User Break Controller (UBC)
41416: 
41417: � Example of sequence of instructions with a branch (however, the example of a sequence of
41418:     instructions with no branch should be applied when the branch destination of a delayed branch
41419:     instruction is the instruction itself + 4):
41420:              100 Instruction A: BT/S L200 (0 instructions after instruction A)
41421:              102 Instruction B (1 instruction after instruction A, 0 instructions after instruction B)
41422:     L200 200 Instruction C (3 instructions after instruction A, 2 instructions after instruction B)
41423:              202 Instruction D (4 instructions after instruction A, 3 instructions after instruction B)
41424: 
41425: 20.3.3 User Break Operation Sequence
41426: 
41427: The sequence of operations from setting of break conditions to user break exception handling is
41428: described below.
41429: 
41430: 1. Specify pre- or post-execution breaking in the case of an instruction access, inclusion or
41431:     exclusion of the data bus value in the break conditions in the case of an operand access, and
41432:     use of independent or sequential channel A and B break conditions, in the break control
41433:     register (BRCR). Set the break addresses in the break address registers for each channel
41434:     (BARA, BARB), the ASIDs corresponding to the break space in the break ASID registers
41435:     (BASRA, BASRB), and the address and ASID masking methods in the break address mask
41436:     registers (BAMRA, BAMRB). If the data bus value is to be included in the break conditions,
41437:     also set the break data in the break data register (BDRB) and the data mask in the break data
41438:     mask register (BDMRB).
41439: 
41440: 2. Set the break bus conditions in the break bus cycle registers (BBRA, BBRB). If even one of
41441:     the BBRA/BBRB instruction access/operand access select (ID bit) and read/write select groups
41442:     (RW bit) is set to 00, a user break interrupt will not be generated on the corresponding channel.
41443:     Make the BBRA and BBRB settings after all other break-related register settings have been
41444:     completed. If breaks are enabled with BBRA/BBRB while the break address, data, or mask
41445:     register, or the break control register is in the initial state after a reset, a break may be
41446:     generated inadvertently.
41447: 
41448: 3. The operation when a break condition is satisfied depends on the BL bit (in the CPU's SR
41449:     register). When the BL bit is 0, exception handling is started and the condition match flag
41450:     (CMFA/CMFB) for the respective channel is set for the matched condition. When the BL bit is
41451:     1, the condition match flag (CMFA/CMFB) for the respective channel is set for the matched
41452:     condition but exception handling is not started.
41453:     The condition match flags (CMFA, CMFB) are set by a branch condition match, but are not
41454:     automatically cleared. Therefore, a memory store instruction should be used on the BRCR
41455: 
41456:                                                                            Rev.7.00 Oct. 10, 2008 Page 865 of 1074
41457:                                                                                                            REJ09B0366-0700
41458: Section 20 User Break Controller (UBC)
41459: 
41460:     register to clear the flags to 0. See section 20.3.6, Condition Match Flag Setting, for the exact
41461:     setting conditions for the condition match flags.
41462: 
41463: 4. When sequential condition mode has been selected, and the channel B condition is matched
41464:     after the channel A condition has been matched, a break is effected at the instruction at which
41465:     the channel B condition was matched. See section 20.3.8, Contiguous A and B Settings for
41466:     Sequential Conditions, for the operation when the channel A condition match and channel B
41467:     condition match occur close together. With sequential conditions, only the channel B condition
41468:     match flag is set. When sequential condition mode has been selected, if it is wished to clear the
41469:     channel A match when the channel A condition has been matched but the channel B condition
41470:     has not yet been matched, this can be done by writing 0 to the SEQ bit in the BRCR register.

